/-!
# Testing J-2 on module-finite algebras

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07PC, lemma-J-2, implication (3) to (1),
and Tag 07P7, definition-J (blueprint: lem-per-j2-finite-algebra-criterion).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

/-- If every module-finite algebra over a Noetherian ring has open regular locus, then
so does every finite type algebra. This is the J-2 clause of `IsQuasiExcellent`, expanded.

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tag 07PC, lemma-J-2, implications (3) to (4)
to (1), and Tag 07P7, definition-J (blueprint: lem-per-j2-finite-algebra-criterion).

Proof (the six steps of the node):
1. Fix a prime `p` of `R` and a finite purely inseparable extension `L` of its residue
   field `k`. Set `A = R/p`. The fraction description of localization identifies `k`
   with the fraction field of `A`: both equality relations for fractions reduce to
   equality of cross products modulo `p`, by primality. Thus `A` embeds in `L`
   (`def-weil-pbc-localization-at-prime`).
2. In characteristic zero, `L = k`, so take `B = A`. In characteristic `p > 0`, choose
   a finite `k`-basis `αᵢ` of `L`. Write `αᵢ ^ nᵢ = cᵢ/dᵢ`, where `nᵢ` is a power
   of `p` and `dᵢ ≠ 0`. Then `βᵢ = dᵢ * αᵢ` satisfies the monic relation
   `βᵢ ^ nᵢ = cᵢ * dᵢ ^ (nᵢ - 1)` in `A`, also when `nᵢ = 1`
   (`def-weil-pbc-finite-purely-inseparable-extension`).
3. Take `B = A[βᵢ]` inside `L`. Reducing powers with those relations shows that the
   bounded monomials span `B` over `A`, hence over `R`. Its fraction field contains
   `k` and all `αᵢ = βᵢ/dᵢ`, so equals `L`. In either characteristic `B` is a domain
   finite as an `R`-module (`def-weil-finitely-generated-module`).
4. By `lem-weil-pbc-finite-algebra-noetherian`, `B` is Noetherian. The hypothesis
   `hfinite` says its regular locus is open.
5. This open contains the zero prime: its localization is the field `L`. A field is
   Noetherian local of dimension zero, with maximal ideal generated by the empty
   family, hence regular local (`def-weil-regular-scheme`).
6. These models satisfy `lem-exc-j2-residue-field-criterion`, which gives the open
   regular locus of every finite type algebra, precisely the J-2 condition of
   `def-exc-excellent-ring`. When `R` is zero there are no primes, so the residue-field
   hypothesis is vacuous and the same criterion applies.
-/
theorem isOpen_regularLocus_of_finite_algebras
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R]
    (hfinite : ∀ (B : Type (max u v)) [CommRing B] [Algebra R B] [Module.Finite R B],
      IsOpen {q : PrimeSpectrum B | IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime q.asIdeal)})
    (C : Type (max u v)) [CommRing C] [Algebra R C] [Algebra.FiniteType R C] :
    IsOpen {q : PrimeSpectrum C | IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime q.asIdeal)} := by
  sorry
